5. Before you commit

One important decision remains: Do you want the free version to be fully functional, with companies paying for alternative licensing terms, or do you want to reserve certain features for paying customers?

A. Same features, two licenses

The Community and Commercial editions have the same core functionality. Customers pay for different licensing terms, support, and contractual benefits.

B. Free Community Edition plus paid premium features

The free version remains useful, while advanced enterprise features are sold separately under commercial terms.

C. Hybrid model

The core is dual-licensed, with additional paid enterprise features, support, and integrations.

For WHAXON, I choose: Hybrid: same core under dual license plus paid enterprise add-ons. Help me define the licensing structure and repository files.
WHAXON: Licensing and repository structure

For your chosen hybrid model, I'd structure WHAXON around three components:

Community Core: the main platform, released under AGPL-3.0 and also available under a commercial license.

Enterprise Add-ons: proprietary modules sold separately, with features for teams and organizations.

Commercial Services: paid support, deployment assistance, custom integrations, and maintenance.

This gives individual users access to the core while creating revenue opportunities from commercial licensing and enterprise capabilities.

One key distinction: AGPL-3.0 does not prohibit commercial use. Anyone can use the community version commercially if they comply with its terms. Your paid license offers alternative contractual rights, not an exclusive right to use WHAXON commercially. 
