6. Suggested rollout

Establish ownership and licensing

Confirm who owns the existing code and choose the legal entity or individual that will offer commercial licenses.

Publish the Community license

Add the official AGPL-3.0 text and clearly identify which source files it covers.

Set up contributor permissions

Adopt a CLA that explicitly covers dual licensing before accepting external contributions. A CLA needs careful drafting; it is not a substitute for the license itself.

Separate commercial modules

Define a clear technical boundary between the core and proprietary add-ons, and review how they interact with AGPL-covered code.

Prepare commercial terms

Define pricing, permitted usage, support, renewal, and redistribution rights before selling licenses.

My final recommendation

Start with AGPL-3.0-or-later for the Community Core, a separately negotiated commercial license for the same core, and proprietary enterprise add-ons.

Before you publish, have a lawyer familiar with software licensing review the dual-license arrangement, CLA, and plugin architecture. The biggest risk is accepting contributions or integrating third-party code without securing the rights needed for your intended commercial model.

